{"id":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods#thm:dc-ch3-3-7","title":"thm:dc-ch3-3-7","path":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods.md","line":790,"body":"","kind":"theorem","status":"not_started","tex":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods.tex#thm:dc-ch3-3-7","uses":["[[references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods#prop:dc-ch3-2-7]]","[[references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods#lem:dc-ch3-3-7-descent]]","[[references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods#lem:dc-ch3-3-7-descent-velocity]]","[[references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods#lem:dc-ch3-3-7-pairmap]]","[[references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods#lem:dc-ch3-3-7-c1pair]]","[[references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods#lem:dc-ch3-3-7-invertible]]"],"inspired_by":[],"bindings":[],"is_index":false,"body_html":"","formal":[],"tex_content":{"path":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods.tex","label":"thm:dc-ch3-3-7","line":2294,"source":"\\begin{theorem}[totally normal neighborhood]\n  \\label{thm:dc-ch3-3-7}\n\n  \\uses{prop:dc-ch3-2-7, lem:dc-ch3-3-7-descent, lem:dc-ch3-3-7-descent-velocity, lem:dc-ch3-3-7-pairmap, lem:dc-ch3-3-7-c1pair}\n\n  \\dcref{ch3:3.7}\n  For any $p\\in M$ there exist a neighborhood $W$ of $p$ and a number $\\delta>0$\n  such that for every $q\\in W$, $\\exp_q$ is a diffeomorphism on\n  $B_\\delta(0)\\subset T_qM$ and $\\exp_q(B_\\delta(0))\\supset W$; that is, $W$ is a\n  normal neighborhood of each of its points.\n\\end{theorem}\n\\begin{proof}\n  \\sketch\n\n  \\uses{lem:dc-ch3-3-7-descent, lem:dc-ch3-3-7-descent-velocity, lem:dc-ch3-3-7-pairmap, lem:dc-ch3-3-7-c1pair, lem:dc-ch3-3-7-invertible}\n  Let $\\varepsilon$, $V$, $\\mathcal{U}$ be as in \\cref{prop:dc-ch3-2-7}, with\n  $\\mathcal{U}\\subset TU$, $V\\subset\\mathbf{x}(U)$. Define $F:\\mathcal{U}\\to M\\times M$\n  by $F(q,v)=(q,\\exp_q v)$. Around $F(p,0)=(p,p)$ take coordinates\n  $(U\\times U;\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{x})$; then since $(d\\exp_p)_0=I$,\n\n  $$\n  dF_{(p,0)}=\\begin{pmatrix}I & I\\\\ 0 & I\\end{pmatrix},\n  $$\n\n  so $F$ is a local diffeomorphism near $(p,0)$, mapping a neighborhood\n  $\\mathcal{U}'=\\{(q,v);\\ q\\in V',\\ v\\in T_qM,\\ |v|<\\delta\\}$ diffeomorphically onto\n  a neighborhood $W'$ of $(p,p)$. Choose $W\\ni p$ with $W\\times W\\subset W'$. For\n  $q\\in W$ and $B_\\delta(0)\\subset T_qM$, $F(\\{q\\}\\times B_\\delta(0))\\supset\\{q\\}\\times W$,\n  so by definition of $F$, $\\exp_q(B_\\delta(0))\\supset W$.\n\\end{proof}","html":" <p></p><section class=\"theorem\"><div class=\"theorem-label\">Theorem</div></section>[totally normal neighborhood] <span class=\"tex-label\" id=\"tex-label-74686d3a64632d6368332d332d37\"></span> <p></p><a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FDoCarmo%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods%23prop%3Adc-ch3-2-7\">↗ prop:dc-ch3-2-7</a> <a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FDoCarmo%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods%23lem%3Adc-ch3-3-7-descent\">↗ lem:dc-ch3-3-7-descent</a> <a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FDoCarmo%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods%23lem%3Adc-ch3-3-7-descent-velocity\">↗ lem:dc-ch3-3-7-descent-velocity</a> <a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FDoCarmo%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods%23lem%3Adc-ch3-3-7-pairmap\">↗ lem:dc-ch3-3-7-pairmap</a> <a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FDoCarmo%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods%23lem%3Adc-ch3-3-7-c1pair\">↗ lem:dc-ch3-3-7-c1pair</a> <p></p><span class=\"tex-source-references\" role=\"list\" style=\"display:block\"><span class=\"tex-dcref\" role=\"listitem\" style=\"display:block\">1. ch3:3.7</span></span>For any <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$p\\in M$\">$p\\in M$</span> there exist a neighborhood <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$W$\">$W$</span> of <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$p$\">$p$</span> and a number <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$\\delta &gt;0$\">$\\delta &gt;0$</span> such that for every <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$q\\in W$\">$q\\in W$</span>, <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$\\exp _q$\">$\\exp _q$</span> is a diffeomorphism on <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$B_\\delta (0)\\subset T_qM$\">$B_\\delta (0)\\subset T_qM$</span> and <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$\\exp _q(B_\\delta (0))\\supset W$\">$\\exp _q(B_\\delta (0))\\supset W$</span>; that is, <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$W$\">$W$</span> is a normal neighborhood of each of its points.  <section class=\"theorem proof\"><div class=\"theorem-label\">Proof</div><p>\\sketch </p><p><a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FDoCarmo%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods%23lem%3Adc-ch3-3-7-descent\">↗ lem:dc-ch3-3-7-descent</a> <a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FDoCarmo%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods%23lem%3Adc-ch3-3-7-descent-velocity\">↗ lem:dc-ch3-3-7-descent-velocity</a> <a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FDoCarmo%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods%23lem%3Adc-ch3-3-7-pairmap\">↗ lem:dc-ch3-3-7-pairmap</a> <a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FDoCarmo%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods%23lem%3Adc-ch3-3-7-c1pair\">↗ lem:dc-ch3-3-7-c1pair</a> <a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FDoCarmo%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods%23lem%3Adc-ch3-3-7-invertible\">↗ lem:dc-ch3-3-7-invertible</a> Let <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$\\varepsilon $\">$\\varepsilon $</span>, <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$V$\">$V$</span>, <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$\\mathcal{U}$\">$\\mathcal{U}$</span> be as in <a class=\"reference tex-ref\" href=\"#node=references%2FDoCarmo%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods%23prop%3Adc-ch3-2-7\" data-tex-node=\"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods#prop:dc-ch3-2-7\" data-tex-anchor=\"tex-label-70726f703a64632d6368332d322d37\">item 2</a>, with <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$\\mathcal{U}\\subset TU$\">$\\mathcal{U}\\subset TU$</span>, <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$V\\subset \\mathbf{x}(U)$\">$V\\subset \\mathbf{x}(U)$</span>. Define <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$F:\\mathcal{U}\\to M\\times M$\">$F:\\mathcal{U}\\to M\\times M$</span> by <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$F(q,v)=(q,\\exp _q v)$\">$F(q,v)=(q,\\exp _q v)$</span>. Around <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$F(p,0)=(p,p)$\">$F(p,0)=(p,p)$</span> take coordinates <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$(U\\times U;\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{x})$\">$(U\\times U;\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{x})$</span>; then since <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$(d\\exp _p)_0=I$\">$(d\\exp _p)_0=I$</span>, </p><p><span class=\"math\" data-display=\"true\" data-tex=\"\\[  dF_{(p,0)}=\\begin{pmatrix} I \n\n&amp;  I\n\n\\\\ 0 \n\n&amp;  I\n\n\\end{pmatrix},  \\]\">\\[  dF_{(p,0)}=\\begin{pmatrix} I \n\n&amp;  I\n\n\\\\ 0 \n\n&amp;  I\n\n\\end{pmatrix},  \\]</span></p><p>so <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$F$\">$F$</span> is a local diffeomorphism near <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$(p,0)$\">$(p,0)$</span>, mapping a neighborhood <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$\\mathcal{U}&#x27;=\\{ (q,v);\\  q\\in V&#x27;,\\  v\\in T_qM,\\  |v|&lt;\\delta \\} $\">$\\mathcal{U}&#x27;=\\{ (q,v);\\  q\\in V&#x27;,\\  v\\in T_qM,\\  |v|&lt;\\delta \\} $</span> diffeomorphically onto a neighborhood <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$W&#x27;$\">$W&#x27;$</span> of <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$(p,p)$\">$(p,p)$</span>. Choose <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$W\\ni p$\">$W\\ni p$</span> with <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$W\\times W\\subset W&#x27;$\">$W\\times W\\subset W&#x27;$</span>. For <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$q\\in W$\">$q\\in W$</span> and <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$B_\\delta (0)\\subset T_qM$\">$B_\\delta (0)\\subset T_qM$</span>, <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$F(\\{ q\\} \\times B_\\delta (0))\\supset \\{ q\\} \\times W$\">$F(\\{ q\\} \\times B_\\delta (0))\\supset \\{ q\\} \\times W$</span>, so by definition of <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$F$\">$F$</span>, <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$\\exp _q(B_\\delta (0))\\supset W$\">$\\exp _q(B_\\delta (0))\\supset W$</span>. </p><span class=\"qed\" aria-label=\"证毕\" style=\"display:block;text-align:right\">□</span></section>","warnings":["部分 TeX 排版细节未完整还原（已保留可读内容）: sketch"]},"previous":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods#lem:dc-ch3-3-7-invertible","next":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods#rem:dc-ch3-3-8","relations":[{"kind":"uses","id":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods#prop:dc-ch3-2-7","title":"prop:dc-ch3-2-7","label":"prop:dc-ch3-2-7"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch0